onsibility and lead through the club\'s work. Starting from financial sponsorship to ancillary support, the university authorities are always by our side.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Our YES Club is basically a \'student wing\' of Transparency International Bangladesh (TIB). Therefore, many of our activities are carried out in collaboration with TIB, which makes our work more meaningful.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">In the last few years, we have done various things including seminars, workshops, campaigns, awareness programs, anti-corruption plays. For example, we did a play at the club fair last semester, the topic was market syndicates.</span></div>','1760083517NETQKUNi.jpg',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,77,NULL,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',34,0,NULL,'2026-03-08 03:10:02','2026-03-07 21:10:02','How UAP\'s club changes us','My journey with the Youth Engagement and Support, or YES Club in short, began in my first year. I have been with the club for more than three and a half years. Although it seemed like just a club in the beginning, this organization has become like my family with time.'),
(537,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Asha University Bangladesh launches Research and Innovation Cell','4454545445','article','Asha University Bangladesh launches Research and Innovation Cell',0,1,1,0,1,1,0,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The private Asha University Bangladesh (ASAUB) has officially inaugurated the ‘Research and Innovation Cell’, which aims to further strengthen the culture of research and innovation in the country’s higher education. The inauguration ceremony was held at the university’s IQAC conference room at 11 am today, Tuesday. Later, the ribbon cutting ceremony was held on the first floor to announce the official inauguration of the cell.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The chief guest was Md. Belal Hossain, Vice-Chancellor of Sher-e-Bangla Agricultural University. Md. Ali Akbar, former Vice-Chancellor of Bangladesh Agricultural University and Kazi Shahdat Kabir, Vice-Chancellor of the private City University. In addition, Professor Mohammad Faruk, a member of the Board of Trustees of Asha University Bangladesh, was present. The ceremony was presided over by SM Rezaul Karim, Vice-Chancellor of Asha University Bangladesh. The private Asha University Bangladesh (ASAUB) has officially inaugurated the ‘Research and Innovation Cell’, which aims to further strengthen the culture of research and innovation in the country’s higher education. The inaugural ceremony was held at 11 am today at the university\'s IQAC conference room. Later, the official inauguration of the cell was announced by cutting the ribbon on the first floor.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The chief guest was Sher-e-Bangla Agricultural University\'s Vice-Chancellor Md. Belal Hossain. Former Vice-Chancellor of Bangladesh Agricultural University Md. Ali Akbar and Vice-Chancellor of private City University Kazi Shahdat Kabir were present as special guests. In addition, Professor Mohammad Faruk, a member of the Board of Trustees of Asha University Bangladesh, was present. The ceremony was presided over by SM Rezaul Karim, Vice-Chancellor of Asha University Bangladesh. The private Asha University Bangladesh (ASAUB) has officially inaugurated the \'Research and Innovation Cell\', which aims to further strengthen the culture of research and innovation in the country\'s higher education. The inaugural ceremony was held at 11 am today at the university\'s IQAC conference room. Later, the official inauguration of the cell was announced by cutting the ribbon on the first floor.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The chief guest was Sher-e-Bangla Agricultural University\'s Vice-Chancellor Md. Belal Hossain. Former Vice-Chancellor of Bangladesh Agricultural University Md. Ali Akbar and Vice-Chancellor of private City University Kazi Shahdat Kabir were present as special guests. In addition, Professor Mohammad Faruk, member of the Board of Trustees of Asha University Bangladesh, was present. The program was presided over by Asha University Bangladesh\'s Vice-Chancellor S. M. Rezaul Karim.</span></div>','1760083653bpudnlDl.jpg',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,77,NULL,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',19,0,NULL,'2026-03-07 07:49:09','2026-03-07 01:49:09','Asha University Bangladesh launches Research and Innovation Cell','The private Asha University Bangladesh (ASAUB) has officially inaugurated the ‘Research and Innovation Cell’, which aims to further strengthen the culture of research and innovation in the country’s higher education. The inauguration ceremony was held at the university’s IQAC conference room at 11 am today, Tuesday. Later, the ribbon cutting ceremony was held on the first floor to announce the official inauguration of the cell.'),
(538,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'ISU Football Fest, Season 1 finals held','4477474477','article','ISU Football Fest, Season 1 finals held',0,1,1,0,1,1,0,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Binary Blasters have emerged as champions of the ISU Football Fest, Season-1 organized by the International Standard University (ISU) Sports Club. ISU Vice-Chancellor Abdul Awal Khan and former national football team player Kaiser Hamid handed over the trophies to the champion and runner-up teams at the Royal Multisports Arena in the capital on Monday afternoon.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Binary Blasters, a team from the Computer Science and Engineering department, defeated Textile Warriors, a team from the ISU Textile Engineering department, by a 2-1 goal margin in a thrilling final match. Md. Atiq Rahman of Merchant Mavericks of the Apparel Merchandising and Management department was named the best player of the tournament, while Mehedi Hasan of Textile Warriors won the best goalkeeper award. Binary Blasters, a team from the International Standard University (ISU) Sports Club, have emerged as champions of the ISU Football Fest, Season-1 organized by the International Standard University (ISU) Sports Club. ISU Vice Chancellor Abdul Awal Khan and former national football team player Kaiser Hamid handed over the trophies to the champion and runner-up teams at the Royal Multisports Arena in the capital on Monday afternoon.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Binary Blasters, a team from the Computer Science and Engineering department, defeated Textile Warriors, a team from the ISU Textile Engineering department, by a 2-1 goal margin in a thrilling final match. Md. Atiq Rahman of Merchant Mavericks, an Apparel Merchandising and Management department, won the best player of the tournament, and Mehedi Hasan of Textile Warriors won the best goalkeeper award. Binary Blasters became the champions of the ISU Football Fest, Season-1, organized by the International Standard University (ISU) Sports Club. ISU Vice Chancellor Abdul Awal Khan and former national football team player Kaiser Hamid handed over the trophies to the champion and runner-up teams at the Royal Multisports Arena in the capital on Monday afternoon.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Binary Blasters, a team from the Computer Science and Engineering department, defeated Textile Warriors, a team from the ISU Textile Engineering department, by a 2-1 goal margin in a thrilling final match to become the champion. Md. Atiq Rahman of Merchant Mavericks, an apparel merchandising and management department, was named the best player of the tournament, while Mehedi Hasan of the Textile Warriors won the best goalkeeper award.</span></div>','1760083736VMJwDFoK.jpg',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,77,NULL,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',18,0,NULL,'2026-03-07 07:49:08','2026-03-07 01:49:08','ISU Football Fest, Season 1 finals held','Binary Blasters have emerged as champions of the ISU Football Fest, Season-1 organized by the International Standard University (ISU) Sports Club. ISU Vice-Chancellor Abdul Awal Khan and former national football team player Kaiser Hamid handed over the trophies to the champion and runner-up teams at the Royal Multisports Arena in the capital on Monday afternoon.'),
(539,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Ilisha from the banned period is being sold openly in Rajbari','44555454','video','Ilisha from the banned period is being sold openly in Rajbari',0,1,1,0,1,1,1,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">The administration has been in a strict position since the morning of Saturday (October 4), the first day of the ban. The naval police and the coast guard will work with the administration to make the campaign successful. The Fisheries Department, including the Goalanda Upazila Nirbahi Officer (UNO), have been conducting exercises in the river since the morning. They are conducting campaigns whenever they see a boat or trawler fishing for hilsa anywhere.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">According to the Fisheries Department sources, there is a 57-kilometer section of the Padma River in Rajbari district. In addition, the Gorai, Harai and Chandana rivers flow through this district. To implement the ban on fishing, propaganda has been carried out in various areas of Rajbari, Goalanda, Kalukhali and Pangsha upazilas on the ban on catching hilsa. In addition to distributing leaflets to fishing villages to comply with the ban, miking has been done in the riverbanks. Since there is no work during this time, the government will provide 25 kg of VGF rice to fishermen involved in hilsa harvesting. The administration has been in a strict position since the morning of Saturday (October 4), the first day of the ban. The naval police and the coast guard will work with the administration to make the campaign successful. The Fisheries Department along with the Goalanda Upazila Executive Officer (UNO) has been conducting exercises in the river since morning. They launch operations whenever they see a boat or trawler fishing for hilsa anywhere.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">According to the Fisheries Department sources, there is a 57-kilometer section of the Padma River in Rajbari district. In addition, the Gorai, Harai and Chandana rivers flow through this district. To implement the ban on fishing, propaganda campaigns have been carried out in various areas of Rajbari, Goalanda, Kalukhali and Pangsha upazilas to enforce the ban on catching hilsa. In addition to distributing leaflets in the fishing villages, miking has been done in the riverbanks. Since there is no work during this time, the government will provide 25 kg of VGF rice to fishermen involved in hilsa harvesting. The administration has been in a strict position since the morning of Saturday (October 4), the first day of the ban. The Naval Police and Coast Guard will work with the administration to make the campaign successful. The Fisheries Department along with the Goalanda Upazila Executive Officer (UNO) has been conducting exercises in the river since morning. They launch raids whenever they see a boat or trawler fishing for Hilsa.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">According to the Fisheries Department, there is a 57-kilometer section of the Padma River in Rajbari district. In addition, the Gorai, Harai and Chandana rivers flow through this district. To implement the ban on fishing, propaganda campaigns are being carried out in various areas of Rajbari, Goalanda, Kalukhali and Pangsha upazilas to enforce the ban on catching Hilsa. In addition to distributing leaflets to fishing villages to enforce the ban, miking has been done in the riverbank areas. Since there is no work at this time, the government will provide 25 kg of VGF rice to fishermen involved in harvesting Hilsa.</span></div>','1760083853IA78dBqG.jpg',NULL,NULL,'','4_IuuH16X5E',NULL,73,82,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',11,0,NULL,'2026-03-12 08:17:16','2026-03-12 02:17:16','Ilisha from the banned period is being sold openly in Rajbari','The administration has been in a strict position since the morning of Saturday (October 4), the first day of the ban. The naval police and the coast guard will work with the administration to make the campaign successful. The Fisheries Department, including the Goalanda Upazila Nirbahi Officer (UNO), have been conducting exercises in the river since the morning. They are conducting campaigns whenever they see a boat or trawler fishing for hilsa anywhere.'),
(540,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Mother and daughter murdered after entering house in Lakshmipur','4554455454','video','Mother and daughter murdered after entering house in Lakshmipur',0,1,1,0,1,1,1,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">According to police and local sources, the mother and daughter were in the second floor of their house on Thursday night. At that time, miscreants entered the house and attacked them. At one stage, the miscreants slit the throats of the mother and daughter in one of the rooms and fled.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Farhad Hossain, the eldest son of the deceased Julekha, said that he came home from his father\'s business at around 10 pm. When he arrived, he found the door of the ground floor open and the furniture in the house in disarray. He called his mother and went up to the second floor. He opened the door of his mother\'s room and saw the bloody bodies of his mother and sister lying on the floor.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">When asked about it, Raipur Circle Assistant Superintendent of Police Jamilul Haque said that several police teams have been stationed in the village after the incident. The exact reason for the incident is not yet known. According to police and local sources, the mother and daughter were in the second floor of their house on Thursday night. At that time, miscreants entered the house and attacked them. At one stage, the miscreants slit the throats of the mother and daughter in one of the rooms and fled.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Farhad Hossain, the eldest son of the deceased Julekha, said that he came home from his father\'s business at around 10 pm. When he arrived, he found the ground floor door open and the furniture in the house in disarray. He called his mother and went up to the second floor. He opened the door of his mother\'s room and saw the bloody bodies of his mother and sister lying on the floor.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">When asked about it, Raipur Circle Assistant Superintendent of Police Jamilul Haque said that several police teams have been stationed in the village after the incident. The exact reason for the incident is not yet known.</span></div>','1760083935F5nusbWA.jpg',NULL,NULL,'','0q8lKZvhF5s',NULL,73,82,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',15,0,NULL,'2026-03-11 14:20:22','2026-03-11 08:20:22','Mother and daughter murdered after entering house in Lakshmipur','According to police and local sources, the mother and daughter were in the second floor of their house on Thursday night. At that time, miscreants entered the house and attacked them. At one stage, the miscreants slit the throats of the mother and daughter in one of the rooms and fled.'),
(541,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'World Egg Day is being celebrated across the country with various events','344343342342432','video','World Egg Day is being celebrated across the country with various events',0,1,1,0,1,1,1,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Protein is one of the essential elements in the global food system. In this regard, eggs are said to be the most readily available, affordable and nutritious source. The importance of eggs in the good health of mankind, child growth and brain development is immense. The International Egg Commission (IEC), a UK-based international organization, was formed in 1964 to establish eggs as a high-quality and easily available non-vegetarian food in the world. Currently, the number of members of this organization is 80. The organization organized the first \'World Egg Day\' in Vienna, the capital of Austria, in 1996 with the aim of meeting the demand for animal protein, building a healthy and talented nation and above all, increasing public awareness about the quality of eggs, which has since been celebrated worldwide on the second Friday of October every year. In continuation of this, World Egg Day is being celebrated in Bangladesh today. This year, the theme of World Egg Day is \'Strong Eggs - Full of Natural Nutrition\' - the Department of Livestock, Bangladesh Poultry Industries Central Council (BPICC) and WAPSA-BBI are jointly celebrating World Egg Day-2025. The main objective of celebrating World Egg Day is to make people around the world aware of the nutritional value of eggs, highlight the role of eggs in providing high-quality protein and essential nutrients at an affordable price, and create public awareness about the need to include eggs in the diet of people of all ages and classes.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Eggs are a delicious and easily available food, which is considered a complete food. It contains 13 essential nutrients for our body. Eggs are one of the most affordable animal proteins. Eggs are called \'poor people\'s protein\'. Again, its place among sustainable animal proteins is at the top of all. That is why it is also called \'nutrient pill\'. The high-quality protein, vitamins, minerals and amino acids in eggs play an essential role in the balanced development of the body. It not only increases immunity, but also helps in increasing brain function and attention. Therefore, including eggs in the daily diet means ensuring an easily available and natural source of nutrition.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Currently, the rate of malnutrition, maternal and neonatal mortality rates have decreased in Bangladesh. The number of stunted and underweight children under the age of five has also decreased. On the contrary, the average life expectancy of people has increased. 30-40 years ago, the number of eggs consumed per capita in our country was only 10 to 15 per year on average. According to the Department of Livestock, this number is currently 137. About 66.8 million eggs are produced daily in the country. By 2031, the target of egg consumption has been set at 165 per person per year.</span></div>','1760084027HhAnEkF0.jpg',NULL,NULL,'','lutP44H4a4o',NULL,73,NULL,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',11,0,NULL,'2026-03-12 07:57:51','2026-03-12 01:57:51','World Egg Day is being celebrated across the country with various events','Eggs are a delicious and easily available food, which is considered a complete food. It contains 13 essential nutrients for our body. Eggs are one of the most affordable animal proteins. Eggs are called \'poor people\'s protein\'. Again, its place among sustainable animal proteins is at the top of all. That is why it is also called \'nutrient pill\'. The high-quality protein, vitamins, minerals and amino acids in eggs play an essential role in the balanced development of the body. It not only increases immunity, but also helps in increasing brain function and attention. Therefore, including eggs in the daily diet means ensuring an easily available and natural source of nutrition.'),
(542,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Vegetable prices slightly lower in capital\'s markets','4454554','video','Vegetable prices slightly lower in capital\'s markets',0,1,1,0,1,1,1,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Moazzem Hossain, a private sector employee, came to the capital\'s Shantinagar Bazaar to shop on the weekend. He said, the price of each vegetable is 80 to 100 taka, and some vegetables are even more expensive. Vegetables have been sold at excessively high prices for the past four months. Ordinary buyers are not able to buy vegetables at such high prices. Where I used to buy one kilogram of vegetables, now I buy half a kilogram of vegetables due to the excessive price. Ordinary buyers are in the same situation as me.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Shahidul Islam, another buyer at Malibag Bazaar, said, for the past four months, vegetable sellers have been saying that most vegetables are not in season now, so the prices are high. But even after four months, the prices of vegetables are still high. So when does the season start and when does it end? Despite the high price of vegetables, I have never seen any initiative to monitor the market.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Regarding the increase in vegetable prices, Ershad Ali, a vegetable seller at Moghbazar, said, \"The field crops have been damaged due to continuous rain for several days. Due to this, the prices of vegetables have increased slightly. Besides, the prices of those vegetables are going to increase as the season of most vegetables is over.\" New vegetables will start to grow in a few days, and then the prices of vegetables will come down. Currently, the supply of vegetables in the market is less than the demand, which is why the prices of all types of vegetables are increasing. Moazzem Hossain, a private employee, came to the capital\'s Shantinagar market to do business on weekends. He said that the price of each vegetable is 80 to 100 taka, while some vegetables are even more expensive. Vegetables have been sold at excessively high prices for the past four months. Ordinary buyers are not able to buy vegetables at such high prices. Where I used to buy one kilogram of vegetables, now I buy half a kilogram of vegetables because of the excessive price. Ordinary buyers are in the same situation as me.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Shahidul Islam, another buyer at Malibagh market, said that for the past four months, vegetable sellers have been saying that most vegetables are not in season now, so the prices are high. But even after four months, the prices of vegetables are increasing. So when is the season starting and when is it ending? Despite such high vegetable prices, I have never seen any initiative to monitor the market.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Regarding the increase in vegetable prices, Ershad Ali, a vegetable seller in Moghbazar, said that the crops have been damaged due to continuous rain for several days. Due to this, the prices of vegetables have increased slightly. In addition, the prices of most vegetables are going to increase as the season is over. New vegetables will start to emerge in a few days, from then on the prices of vegetables will come down. Currently, the supply of vegetables in the market is less than the demand, which is why the prices of all types of vegetables are going up. Moazzem Hossain, a private employee, came to the capital\'s Shantinagar market to market on weekends. He said that the price of each vegetable is 80 to 100 taka, while some vegetables are even more expensive. Vegetables have been sold at excessively high prices for the past four months. Ordinary buyers are not able to buy vegetables at such high prices. Where I used to buy one kilogram of vegetables, now I buy half a kilogram of vegetables due to the excessively high prices. Ordinary buyers are in the same situation as me.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Shahidul Islam, another buyer in Malibagh market, said that for the past four months, vegetable sellers have been saying that most vegetables are not in season now, so the prices are high. But even after four months, the prices of vegetables are high. So when does the season start and when does it end? Despite the high prices of vegetables, I have never seen any initiative to monitor the market.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Regarding the increase in vegetable prices, Ershad Ali, a vegetable seller in Moghbazar, said that the crops have been damaged due to continuous rain for several days. Due to this, the prices of vegetables have increased a little. In addition, since the season of most vegetables is over, the prices of those vegetables are going to increase. New vegetables will start growing in a few days, and from then on the prices of vegetables will decrease. Currently, the supply of vegetables in the market is less than the demand, which is why the prices of all types of vegetables are going to increase.</span></div>','1760084153Zvxs5c7k.jpg',NULL,NULL,'','i2QyNExef2M',NULL,73,NULL,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',30,0,NULL,'2026-03-12 06:57:50','2026-03-12 00:57:50','Vegetable prices slightly lower in capital\'s markets','Moazzem Hossain, a private sector employee, came to the capital\'s Shantinagar Bazaar to shop on the weekend. He said, the price of each vegetable is 80 to 100 taka, and some vegetables are even more expensive. Vegetables have been sold at excessively high prices for the past four months. Ordinary buyers are not able to buy vegetables at such high prices. Where I used to buy one kilogram of vegetables, now I buy half a kilogram of vegetables due to the excessive price. Ordinary buyers are in the same situation as me.'),
(543,1,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Homes and farmland are disappearing before the eyes of residents','54545454fdfdfd','video','Homes and farmland are disappearing before the eyes of residents',0,1,1,0,1,1,1,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Nazma Begum (46), a housewife from Sherpur Sadar Upazila, was forced to leave her home one night, clutching her four children. She saw her last refuge disappearing into the river before her eyes. In this situation, she did not know where to go or what to do. Expressing her helplessness, this resident of Bhagalghar village said in a tearful voice, \"The Brahmaputra River has washed away our home in front of our eyes. Where will we go with the water we have?\"</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">For now, Nazma has taken shelter in someone else\'s house with her daily wage laborer husband Abdullah and their children. However, she is looking at the darkness in her eyes, wondering where she will go in the future. This pain or helplessness of losing her home in the Brahmaputra River is not only Nazma Begum\'s alone, but also the same plight of nearly a hundred families in Dakshin Bhagalghar village of Charpakshimari Union. The families complain that they have become destitute due to the lack of effective measures taken by the government to prevent erosion. Nazma Begum (46), a housewife from Sherpur Sadar Upazila, was forced to leave her house one night with her four children in her arms. She saw her last shelter disappearing into the river before her eyes. In this situation, she does not know where to go, what to do. Expressing such helplessness, this resident of Bhagalghar village said in a tearful voice, \"The Brahmaputra River has washed away our home in front of our eyes. Where will we go with Polapan?\"</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">For now, Nazma, her daily wage laborer husband Abdullah and their children have taken shelter in someone else\'s house. However, she is looking at the darkness in her eyes, wondering where she will go in the future. This pain or helplessness of losing her home in the Brahmaputra River is not only Nazma Begum\'s alone, but also the same plight of about a hundred families in Dakshin Bhagalghar village of Charpakshimari Union. The families complain that they have become destitute due to the lack of effective measures taken by the government to prevent erosion. Nazma Begum (46), a housewife from Sherpur Sadar Upazila, was forced to leave her house one night with her four children in her arms. She saw her last shelter disappearing into the river before her eyes. In this situation, she does not know where to go, what to do. Expressing such helplessness, this resident of Bhagalghar village said in a tearful voice, \"The Brahmaputra River has washed away our home in front of our eyes. Where will we go with Polapan?\"</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">For now, Nazma, her daily wage laborer husband Abdullah and their children have taken shelter in someone else\'s house. However, she is looking at the darkness in her eyes, wondering where she will go in the future. This pain or helplessness of losing her home in the Brahmaputra River is not only Nazma Begum\'s alone, but also the same plight of about a hundred families in Dakshin Bhagalghar village of Charpakshimari Union. The families complain that they have become destitute due to the lack of effective measures taken by the government to prevent erosion.</span></div>','1760084243rFteYtKq.jpg',NULL,NULL,'','V0xMhsb78Ec',NULL,73,NULL,0,NULL,0,1,0,'true',36,0,NULL,'2026-03-12 07:27:52','2026-03-12 01:27:52','Homes and farmland are disappearing before the eyes of residents','Nazma Begum (46), a housewife from Sherpur Sadar Upazila, was forced to leave her home one night, clutching her four children. She saw her last refuge disappearing into the river before her eyes. In this situation, she did not know where to go or what to do. Expressing her helplessness, this resident of Bhagalghar village said in a tearful voice, \"The Brahmaputra River has washed away our home in front of our eyes. Where will we go with the water we have?\"'),
(544,1,15,NULL,NULL,'Job opportunities in various grades including 9th in Bangladesh Hi-Tech Park','554454','article','Job opportunities in various grades including 9th in Bangladesh Hi-Tech Park',0,1,1,0,1,1,0,NULL,'<div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Bangladesh Hi-Tech Park has published a recruitment notice for four posts from grades 9 to 16. The recruitment notice was published yesterday, Wednesday.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Name and description of the post</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">1. Assistant Director (Administration)</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Number of posts: 1</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Educational qualifications and experience: Must have a first class master\'s degree or equivalent from a recognized university or a second class master\'s degree with second class honors or equivalent and be proficient in computer operation. Third division or class in any examination is not acceptable.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Pay scale and grade: 22,000-53,060/- (Grade-9)</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">2. Deputy Assistant Engineer (Civil)</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Number of posts: 1</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Educational qualifications and experience: Must have a three-year diploma in civil engineering from a recognized institute or organization and be proficient in computer operation. Third division or class in any examination is not acceptable.</span></div